# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Amos'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'prophets'
SUBTITLE = 'The Herdman’s Warning, the Roaring LORD, and David’s Tabernacle Raised'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Amos is “the words of Amos, who was among the herdmen of Tekoa, which he saw concerning Israel” (Amos 1:1). He begins with the LORD roaring from Zion, speaks judgment over the nations and over Israel, is sent to “my people Israel,” and closes with visions of the end and the promise to raise up the tabernacle of David and plant Israel upon their land (Amos 1:2; 7:15; 9:11, 14–15).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where the LORD speaks to the house of Israel about judgment, a famine of hearing, and a restoration to the land under the raised tabernacle of David (Amos 3:2; 5:4; 8:11; 9:11, 15).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Amos', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — Judgment on the Nations (Chs. 1–2)'), ('p', 'The LORD roars from Zion and pronounces judgment on the nations round about and on Israel (Amos 1:2).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — Hear This Word (Chs. 3–6)'), ('p', 'The only family the LORD has known is told to prepare to meet its God, and is told to seek good and let judgment run down (Amos 3:2–3, 7; 4:12; 5:4, 14, 18, 24).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — The Visions and the Herdman (Chs. 7–8)'), ('p', 'Amos answers Amaziah and is told to prophesy to Israel; the end is come, and a famine of hearing is promised (Amos 7:14–15; 8:2, 11).'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — The Restoration (Ch. 9)'), ('p', 'The LORD will raise up David’s tabernacle, bring again the captivity of Israel, and plant them on their land (Amos 9:11, 14–15).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Amos', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Only Family Known</b> — “You only have I known of all the families of the earth: therefore I will punish you for all your iniquities” (Amos 3:2).', '<b>Walking Together</b> — “Can two walk together, except they be agreed?” (Amos 3:3).', '<b>Prepare to Meet Thy God</b> — “Prepare to meet thy God, O Israel” (Amos 4:12).', '<b>Seek Good</b> — “Seek good, and not evil, that ye may live” (Amos 5:14).', '<b>Justice Like Waters</b> — “Let judgment run down as waters, and righteousness as a mighty stream” (Amos 5:24).', '<b>A Famine of Hearing</b> — “A famine in the land, not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD” (Amos 8:11).', '<b>David’s Tabernacle</b> — “In that day will I raise up the tabernacle of David that is fallen” (Amos 9:11).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Amos', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Amos points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The Tabernacle of David</b> — “In that day will I raise up the tabernacle of David that is fallen” (Amos 9:11) is quoted in Acts at the council at Jerusalem (Acts 15:16–17); both passages are reported as written.', '<b>The Herdman Sent</b> — Amos says, “the LORD took me as I followed the flock” (Amos 7:15).', '<b>The Restored Land</b> — “I will plant them upon their land” (Amos 9:15).'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Amos', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Amos speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Amos operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Amos in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Amos reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['Israel as the people the LORD has known (Amos 3:2)', 'the house of Israel told to seek the LORD (Amos 5:4)', 'a famine of hearing in the land (Amos 8:11)', 'the tabernacle of David raised and the captivity brought again (Amos 9:11, 14)', 'Israel planted upon the land the LORD gave them (Amos 9:15)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Amos that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Amos contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['Concerning Israel — “which he saw concerning Israel.” (Amos 1:1).', 'The LORD Roars From Zion — “The LORD will roar from Zion, and utter his voice from Jerusalem.” (Amos 1:2).', 'The Only Family — “You only have I known of all the families of the earth.” (Amos 3:2).', 'Prepare to Meet Thy God — “prepare to meet thy God, O Israel.” (Amos 4:12).', 'Seek Ye Me — “thus saith the LORD unto the house of Israel, Seek ye me, and ye shall live.” (Amos 5:4).', 'Sent to My People — “Go, prophesy unto my people Israel.” (Amos 7:15).', 'The End Is Come — “The end is come upon my people of Israel.” (Amos 8:2).', 'A Famine of Hearing — “not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD.” (Amos 8:11).', 'David’s Tabernacle — “will I raise up the tabernacle of David that is fallen.” (Amos 9:11).', 'The Captivity Brought Again — “I will bring again the captivity of my people of Israel.” (Amos 9:14).', 'Planted on Their Land — “I will plant them upon their land.” (Amos 9:15).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Amos Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Amos and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Amos teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Amos', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['Concerning Israel', '“which he saw concerning Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Amos 1:1'], ['The LORD Roars From Zion', '“The LORD will roar from Zion, and utter his voice from Jerusalem.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Amos 1:2'], ['The Only Family', '“You only have I known of all the families of the earth.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Amos 3:2'], ['Prepare to Meet Thy God', '“prepare to meet thy God, O Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Amos 4:12'], ['Seek Ye Me', '“thus saith the LORD unto the house of Israel, Seek ye me, and ye shall live.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Amos 5:4'], ['Sent to My People', '“Go, prophesy unto my people Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Amos 7:15'], ['The End Is Come', '“The end is come upon my people of Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Amos 8:2'], ['A Famine of Hearing', '“not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Amos 8:11'], ['David’s Tabernacle', '“will I raise up the tabernacle of David that is fallen.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Amos 9:11'], ['The Captivity Brought Again', '“I will bring again the captivity of my people of Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Amos 9:14'], ['Planted on Their Land', '“I will plant them upon their land.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Amos 9:15']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Amos that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Amos teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['Walking Together', '“Can two walk together, except they be agreed?”', 'Amos 3:3'], ['The Prophets Told', '“the Lord GOD will do nothing, but he revealeth his secret unto his servants the prophets.”', 'Amos 3:7'], ['Seek Good', '“Seek good, and not evil, that ye may live.”', 'Amos 5:14'], ['Justice Like Waters', '“let judgment run down as waters, and righteousness as a mighty stream.”', 'Amos 5:24']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Amos without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Amos in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Walk in agreement</b> — Two walk together if agreed (Amos 3:3).', '<b>Receive what He reveals</b> — He tells His servants the prophets (Amos 3:7).', '<b>Seek good</b> — And not evil (Amos 5:14).', '<b>Let justice run</b> — Like waters and a mighty stream (Amos 5:24).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Amos', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Amos in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['Judgment on the Nations (Chs. 1–2) — The roaring LORD. <i>(Amos 1:2)</i>', 'Hear This Word (Chs. 3–6) — The only family known. <i>(Amos 3:2–3, 7; 4:12; 5:4, 14, 24)</i>', 'The Visions and the Herdman (Chs. 7–8) — A famine of hearing. <i>(Amos 7:14–15; 8:2, 11)</i>', 'The Restoration (Ch. 9) — David’s tabernacle and the land. <i>(Amos 9:11, 14–15)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Amos, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward justice, humility, and attention to the word of the LORD without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Amos simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Amos stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
